[32.001821, "o", "    ✗ bash — exit 1\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 13 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: find / -name \"pip\" -type d 2>/dev/null | head -10 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash find / -name \"pip\" -type d 2>/dev/null | head -10\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 14 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -c \"import ensurepip; print(ensurepip.__file__)\" 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -c \"import ensurepip; print(ensurepip.__file__)\" 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 15 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: ls /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/ 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash ls /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/ 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ The bundled wheel is there. The issue is that `ensurepip --upgrade` think…\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info && python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist"]
[32.001915, "o", "-info &&…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 17 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Now let me verify it works:\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 18 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 19 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Let me do a quick test install to confirm everything works end-to-end:\r\n[approval] bash: pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1 | head -10 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip3 install --dry-run requests 2>&1 | head -10\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 20 tool call(s) so far\r\npip is fully working again.\r\n\r\n**What was wrong:** The `pip` Python package was corrupted — the `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata directory existed in `site-packages`, but the actual `pip/` package directory (the importable module) was missing. This caused `pip3` to fail with `ModuleNotFoundError: No mod"]
[32.001928, "o", "ule named 'pip'`.\r\n\r\n**How it was fixed:** Removed the stale `.dist-info` directory and re-ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, which extracted the bundled `pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl` to restore the missing `pip` module.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=qwen3.6-35b tools=20 surface=76d25613e3da\r\n"]
